<div class="aboutProjectSection1024 testingCase">
    <div class="sectionText testingIntro">
        <h1>Employee Learning Platform</h1>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="aboutProjectSection1024 testingCase">
    <div class="testingSplit testingOverview">
        <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
            <h4>Context</h4>
            <span>At the bank, learning is part of everyday work. Employees take mandatory and recurring courses, build skills, and demonstrate what they know. Managers track their teams' learning, while administrators create programs and manage assignments.</span>
            <span>Many of these processes used to run through WebTutor and other external systems. We began moving them to our own platform to bring learning into one ecosystem and shape it around the bank's needs.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
            <h4>My role</h4>
            <span>I was responsible for the design of the entire LMS: how employees discover and complete learning, and how managers and administrators oversee it. I designed the workflows and connections that made the platform feel like one system.</span>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="testingIntroImages">
    <img src="projects/testing/intro1.png" alt="Employee learning and training budget overview">
    <img src="projects/testing/intro2.png" alt="Banking security course screen">
</div>

<div class="testingStory">
    <div class="testingStoryRow">
        <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
            <h4>One ecosystem, many workflows</h4>
            <span>The LMS brought together many distinct, connected tools. A course could include a test; managers could see completion results; and administrators managed content and assignments in separate interfaces.</span>
            <span>Each service had its own purpose, but together they worked as one system.</span>
        </div>
        <img src="projects/testing/scheme.png" alt="Map of the tools in the LMS platform">
    </div>
    <div class="testingStoryRow">
        <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
            <h4>Understanding how employees learn</h4>
            <span>To understand how employees choose learning opportunities and what gets in their way, the team conducted research.</span>
            <span>We combined seven focus groups with new and experienced employees with an online survey of 1,195 people. This let us test our initial assumptions and identify what actually made learning hard to find and complete.</span>
        </div>
        <img src="projects/testing/discovery.png" alt="Findings from the employee learning research">
    </div>
</div>

<section class="testingCatalogSection">
    <div class="testingCatalogContent">
        <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
            <h4>How research shaped the learning catalog</h4>
            <span>Research showed that employees often heard about learning through email, while resources were scattered across several systems. When choosing a program, they cared about its content, length, format, and relevance to their work.</span>
            <span>These findings shaped the LMS. One clear example was a unified learning catalog.</span>
            <span>We brought courses, academies, workshops, and other formats into one place. Instead of searching across multiple systems, employees had a single starting point.</span>
            <span>Categories, filters, and curated collections made the catalog easier to explore. Program cards and pages showed the content, format, duration, and other details people needed to decide.</span>
        </div>
        <img class="testingCatalogImage" src="projects/testing/catalog.png" alt="Learning catalog interface">
        <div class="testingCatalogPublish">
            <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
                <h4>Giving the catalog its content</h4>
                <span>That meant designing both the employee experience and the administration tools. Administrators could create entries for different types of learning, add descriptions, configure settings, and manage the catalog's content.</span>
            </div>
            <img src="projects/testing/catalog_publish.png" alt="Publishing learning content in the catalog">
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="testingCourseSection">
    <div class="testingCourseContent">
        <img src="projects/testing/course.png" alt="Course player and its contents">
        <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
            <h4>What goes into a course?</h4>
            <span>For an employee, a course is a sequence of learning materials. Creating that sequence, however, requires a separate tool.</span>
            <span>We designed a course builder that could combine information pages, surveys, and tests.</span>
            <span>Administrators created pages in a visual editor, while employees took the finished course in a dedicated player.</span>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<div class="aboutProjectSection1024 testingCase">
    <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
        <h4>Even the questions needed a system of their own</h4>
        <span>At first glance, a survey or test is just a few questions, answer choices, and a Continue button. At the bank, these interactions were backed by two independent services. Teams used them to collect feedback, check knowledge, and support other workflows, including embedding them in courses.</span>
        <span>The complexity lived on the administrator's side: creating different question types, setting completion rules, launching for different audiences, and working with results.</span>
        <span>My job was to make this system flexible and understandable while keeping the employee experience simple.</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="testingIntroImages">
    <img src="projects/testing/test.png" alt="Survey question in a learning course">
    <img src="projects/testing/survey.png" alt="Knowledge check in a learning course">
</div>

<div class="aboutProjectSection1024 testingCase testingCaseBody">
    <div class="testingSplit testingSplitRight">
        <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn">
            <h4>Breaking a complex model into clear entities</h4>
            <span>Both services had a two-level architecture. First, an administrator created a template with questions, types, and order. They could then launch multiple surveys or tests from that template for different audiences and use cases. Each launch had its own settings and unique link. This made content reusable and allowed launches to be created automatically through an API.</span>
            <span>I designed the interface around these entities: separating them, defining the card structure, and connecting them in a coherent workflow.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="testingImagePanel testingModelImage">
            <img src="projects/testing/Testing-model.png" alt="Entity model for surveys and tests">
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="testingSplit testingSplitLeft">
        <div class="testingImagePanel">
            <img src="projects/testing/Testing-fork.png" alt="Testing service built from the surveys foundation">
        </div>
        <div class="sectionText testingTextColumn testingTextColumnRight">
            <h4>A shared architecture sped up development</h4>
            <span>When the team needed a testing service, surveys provided a technical and interface foundation: developers forked the back end and front end.</span>
            <span>The standalone cards, question types, and settings in the design could be extended rather than designed again. We added correct answers, question blocks, passing scores, attempts, and a timer. The new service had its own logic while retaining a familiar structure.</span>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="aboutProjectSection1024 testingCase testingResults">
    <div class="sectionSeparator sectionSeparatorGray"></div>
    <div class="sectionText">
        <h4>Results</h4>
        <span>We built a unified corporate learning ecosystem on the bank's own platform, bringing employee and administration tools together.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="testingResultsGrid">
        <div class="testingResultsColumn">
            <div><strong>One place to start</strong><span>The catalog brought different learning formats together, making opportunities easier to find.</span></div>
            <div><strong>More informed choices</strong><span>Filters and detailed cards helped employees choose programs by content, format, and duration.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Content management</strong><span>Builders let administrators create materials, assemble courses, and configure surveys and tests.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="testingResultsColumn">
            <div><strong>Reusable solutions</strong><span>We designed the services to work independently while also making use of each other's capabilities.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Learning oversight</strong><span>Managers and learning teams gained tools to assign programs, track progress, and work with results.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Less reliance on legacy systems</strong><span>In-house services gradually replaced WebTutor and other external tools, allowing the LMS to evolve with the bank's needs.</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
